# Linux versus Windows — importance to global computing infrastructure.
#
# "More important" is deliberately scoped to systemic dependence: how much of
# the modern computing stack relies on the platform, not which desktop OS is
# more familiar or preferable. The Windows case and Linux's weaknesses are kept
# in the graph so the conclusion is contestable rather than promotional.

question platform-importance
  Which operating-system family is more important to global computing infrastructure?
  expects claim

claim linux-more-important
  Linux is more important than Windows to global computing infrastructure.

claim windows-more-important
  Windows's desktop and organizational reach makes it more important overall.

candidate-for platform-importance
  linux-more-important
  windows-more-important

claim systemic-dependence-standard
  Importance here means how much critical infrastructure and downstream computing depend on the platform.

claim infrastructure-outweighs-desktop
  Foundational infrastructure dependence matters more to this comparison than desktop market presence alone.

claim linux-internet-backbone
  Linux is a foundational platform for internet-facing infrastructure.

claim linux-cloud-platform
  Linux is the default substrate for much cloud-native computing.

claim linux-mobile-embedded
  Linux-derived systems underpin enormous parts of mobile and embedded computing.

claim linux-open-infrastructure
  Linux enables infrastructure that organizations can inspect, adapt, redistribute, and operate independently.

claim linux-scientific-platform
  Linux is foundational to high-performance and research computing.

claim windows-enduser-platform
  Windows remains exceptionally important to end-user and organizational computing.

observation linux-server-deployment
  Linux is widely deployed on production servers across hosting, enterprise, and public infrastructure.

observation web-serving-footprint
  A large share of public web services run on Linux or other Unix-like server stacks.

observation network-appliance-footprint
  Routers, firewalls, storage appliances, and network services frequently use Linux-based systems.

supports linux-internet-backbone
  linux-server-deployment      weight 0.90 estimated
  web-serving-footprint        weight 0.85 estimated
  network-appliance-footprint  weight 0.75 estimated

observation public-cloud-footprint
  Linux images and managed Linux services are central offerings across major public clouds.

observation container-host-standard
  Linux kernel facilities provide the native foundation for mainstream container isolation.

observation kubernetes-node-footprint
  Most production Kubernetes worker nodes use Linux operating systems.

supports linux-cloud-platform
  public-cloud-footprint   weight 0.90 estimated
  container-host-standard  weight 0.95 estimated
  kubernetes-node-footprint weight 0.85 estimated

observation android-kernel-lineage
  Android uses the Linux kernel and extends Linux's reach across billions of mobile devices.

observation embedded-device-footprint
  Linux is common in televisions, vehicles, industrial controllers, consumer devices, and edge computers.

supports linux-mobile-embedded
  android-kernel-lineage      weight 0.95 measured
  embedded-device-footprint   weight 0.80 estimated

observation forkability
  Linux's licensing permits organizations to fork and maintain the system without a single vendor's permission.

observation source-auditability
  Its source can be inspected and modified by operators, researchers, vendors, and governments.

observation cross-org-maintenance
  Development and maintenance are distributed across many companies, communities, and independent contributors.

supports linux-open-infrastructure
  forkability            weight 0.90 measured
  source-auditability    weight 0.85 measured
  cross-org-maintenance  weight 0.75 estimated

observation supercomputer-footprint
  Linux dominates the operating systems used by leading supercomputers.

observation research-cluster-footprint
  Research clusters and technical computing environments commonly standardize on Linux.

supports linux-scientific-platform
  supercomputer-footprint      weight 0.95 measured
  research-cluster-footprint   weight 0.80 estimated

observation windows-desktop-footprint
  Windows has an enormous installed base on personal and workplace computers.

observation office-workflow-dependence
  Many organizations depend on Windows-centered productivity and document workflows.

observation enterprise-identity-dependence
  Active Directory and related Microsoft administration systems remain deeply embedded in enterprises.

observation commercial-app-dependence
  Important commercial creative, engineering, accounting, and specialist applications target Windows first or exclusively.

observation pc-gaming-dependence
  Windows remains the primary compatibility target for much of PC gaming.

observation accessibility-support-depth
  Windows benefits from mature accessibility tooling, hardware support, training, and commercial assistance.

supports windows-enduser-platform
  windows-desktop-footprint       weight 0.95 measured
  office-workflow-dependence      weight 0.85 estimated
  enterprise-identity-dependence  weight 0.85 estimated
  commercial-app-dependence       weight 0.80 estimated
  pc-gaming-dependence            weight 0.75 estimated
  accessibility-support-depth     weight 0.70 estimated

observation linux-fragmentation
  Distribution, packaging, and configuration differences impose real integration and support costs.

observation linux-desktop-app-gap
  Linux desktop users still face gaps in some commercial applications and vendor-certified workflows.

observation uneven-vendor-support
  Hardware and software vendors do not support every Linux distribution equally well.

observation governance-capture-risk
  Open development does not eliminate concentrated influence by major corporate contributors and maintainers.

link linux-fragmentation opposes linux-open-infrastructure
  weight 0.55 estimated

link governance-capture-risk opposes linux-open-infrastructure
  weight 0.45 estimated

link linux-desktop-app-gap supports windows-enduser-platform
  weight 0.70 estimated

link uneven-vendor-support supports windows-enduser-platform
  weight 0.65 estimated

supports linux-more-important
  linux-internet-backbone    weight 0.90 estimated
  linux-cloud-platform       weight 0.95 estimated
  linux-mobile-embedded      weight 0.85 estimated
  linux-open-infrastructure  weight 0.75 estimated
  linux-scientific-platform  weight 0.80 estimated
  systemic-dependence-standard weight 0.85 assumed
  infrastructure-outweighs-desktop weight 0.80 assumed

link windows-enduser-platform supports windows-more-important
  weight 0.90 estimated

link windows-more-important opposes linux-more-important
  weight 0.75 estimated

link infrastructure-outweighs-desktop opposes windows-more-important
  weight 0.80 assumed

analyst holds linux-more-important
  confidence 0.75 estimated
  because systemic-dependence-standard
  note The conclusion is about infrastructural dependence, not desktop quality or total user value.
